### 2.4 Recursive learning units

**Documentation status, 19 November 2026.** The two Skill Graph compendia
describe a proposal for expert review of representative unit assessments.
The documentation revision is authorized. Adoption of the proposal as the
product contract and subject review of the Hessian E.1 example are pending.
The confirmed November contracts in §§2.3–2.4, 4 and 10.2 still govern
curriculum authoring, learner results, runtime work and QA. The Graph Definition
keeps the applicable recognition formulas in [§13.2](docs/concept/skill-graph/assessment-gated-completion.md#sec-transitive-demand)
and [§13.4](docs/concept/skill-graph/assessment-gated-completion.md#sec-global-effect),
and the applicable validity rules R1–R5 in [§13.3](docs/concept/skill-graph/assessment-gated-completion.md#sec-why-graph-not-enough).
“Binding target model” below refers to the confirmed November contracts,
including the 14–15 November ordering and stage-plan decisions; it does not
adopt the separate unit-assessment proposal of 19 November. The [discussion
paper](docs/concept/skill-graph/unit-assessment-discussion-2026-11-19.de.md)
sets out the changes that adoption would require.

**Guided unit entry, Product Owner decision of 22 November 2026.** Each
pedagogical unit with assessments of its own has one atomic orientation goal.
On entering the unit, show its place, an illustrated situation, a short topic
overview and its learning promise. Browsing changes no learner result. Guided
study starts with this goal. Content, exercises and flashcard practice follow
its personal completion or a valid, version-bound planning credit for that
orientation. A learner can speculate, ask a question or go straight to the
closing offer. The coach summarizes the outlook and records the binary result
only after the learner accepts that offer. On return, use existing evidence
and offer the next step. A green check records a personally completed opening.
An indirect credit has a gray check. A preparation waiver grants neither.

Apply the rule to the unit actually entered. Guided descent from a parent
introduces the parent and then the child. Direct entry into a child introduces
the child with a short reference to the parent. Structural groupings give
location without an extra atomic goal. A deliberate direct assessment attempt
can start before orientation. Leaving before submission returns to the guided
path with its outstanding opening. In the E.1 unit assignment, A–D suffice.
The first implementation covers E.1 and its assessed child in Hessen G8/G9.

**Learner-facing hierarchy, 21 November 2026.** The target for a given learner
and scope is one containment hierarchy shared by the Cockpit tree and the
graphical learning map. The same unit has the same children and an atomic
goal opens under the same parent in both views. Prerequisite arrows, source
references and links to practice material do not create an additional owner.
A presentation group containing only a single atomic learning goal must not
create a navigable learning unit around it. Open the goal directly and retain
its context, direct actions and evidence. Keep declared programme stages,
source scope and independent unit promises, assessments and ordering intact.
Verify parity against actual displayed parent paths in the same scope as the
tree and map converge. The learner tree places visible prerequisites and authored unit steps before their successors, including within nested units. Independent siblings retain a stable order.

**Learner-facing evidence marks for all subjects and scopes.** A green check
means the learner has their own successful evidence for this goal. The same check in gray
means that assessed work on another goal gives a
documented credit for this one. On 23 November 2026 the Product Owner removed
the minus sign. An atom with no individual evidence or reviewed credit keeps
its open or active mark, including when the current assignment can finish
without it. Tree, map, counts and accessible labels report this consistently.
Goal and unit tiles offer ordinary navigation without practice buttons.
Show a certified section's completion separately from how many individual
goals have their own evidence. Display choices never write mastery.

**Hessian E.1 pilot decision, 20 November 2026.** For an explicitly selected
unit-qualification assignment, passing the four designated versions A–D ends
the required work for that assignment. An individually open goal remains
available voluntarily or due under a separate explicit assignment. A valid historical part receipt
counts towards its part after a scope change. If it lacks the planning-release
binding at grading, the cockpit and coach explain why the two individual
goals remain open. A separate due assignment takes priority on a request to
continue. Once the unit assignment is complete and no such work is due, offer
a choice of individually open targets of the same unit for voluntary study.
The additional horizontal-throw examination remains available voluntarily and
has its own result. The local acceptance preview opts into the pilot. Its
separate runtime setting defaults to off. The Product Owner reserves the
pedagogical acceptance of E.1 and subsequent acceptance of the Hessian template.

**Section selections in atomic plans, 21 November 2026.** With the unit
assessment pilot enabled, a section selection contributes the goals required
by its applicable assessment policy. This applies when reading an existing
plan as well as creating a new one. An old E.1 section selection that captured
the voluntary horizontal-throw exam contributes 16 positions instead of 17
in the GK example. An explicit selection of that exam still counts, including
in older plans that stored it only once across overlapping blocks. Stored
selections, assessment receipts and individual mastery are preserved. With
the pilot disabled, plans use their stored goal lists.

The Product Owner confirmed **Lerneinheit** (English **learning unit**,
conceptual name `LearningUnit`) on **4 November 2026**. This replaces the former
curricular-level terminology. Personalization Levels 1–4, QA maturity and goal
`level` difficulty retain their meanings.

- A unit states what learners will be able to do and why it matters. A motivational
  introduction leads into goals and optional subunits. Calendar years, semesters
  and navigation headings are not automatically assessed learning units.
- Assign unit roles through authoritative placements or reviewed composition
  structure for a resolved scope/revision. Every acquisition target has one
  direct owner in that hierarchy. Ancestors aggregate child targets; general
  canonical multiple-parent containment is still permitted.
- Derive unit completion from all required child completions and its own
  goal-type results. No extra unit mastery is stored. A parent may add an
  integrative assessment; an aggregate needs no redundant extra examination.
- Local recognition applies only to directly owned ordinary targets under the
  reviewed assessment contract. Never turn a parent's descendant union into a
  second shortcut. Valid direct and implicit competence remains globally effective
  while a later or parent exam is open. Exams and cards always need their own
  explicit results; a parent pass never passes them implicitly.
- **Whole-unit order (B9, 14 November 2026).** Only an explicitly authored
  `after` states the intended full completion of one unit before another
  starts; it is order, never a new whole-unit prerequisite type. Atomic
  prerequisites demand only their competencies. The former undecided access
  profile is retired in the binding target model: `after` blocks no starts
  or direct assessment attempts and changes neither frontier nor mastery.
  Task availability, authentication, scope and session binding remain their
  own contracts. This does not claim removal of historical code paths;
  independent branches may proceed in parallel.
- **Composition child order (16 November 2026).** Source composition format
  `1.2` supports an optional `structure.childOrder.after` list of
  `{unit, after}` pairs naming direct structure children. It declares a partial
  planning order; an empty list declares no additional child order. Actual
  atomic demand still applies and must satisfy E3. Without this
  field, legacy sibling-order behavior remains. Checked stage-plan bindings
  retain priority. Filtering must preserve reachability through removed
  intermediate children and reduce the visible order transitively. Unknown,
  self, duplicate, cyclic or transitively redundant declarations fail
  authoring validation. This changes neither canonical `requires` nor learner
  scope, frontier, mastery or task access. Do not downgrade the new source
  format into an older export contract that cannot retain its order.
  Author a justified partial order, never an adjacency chain copied from the
  display list. Check E3 against actual cross-unit demand: empty declarations
  are not a shortcut for leaving required ordering unexplained. Repair an
  incoherent unit grouping before declaring whole-unit sequencing.
- **Explicit acquisition placement (16 November 2026).** In composition format
  `1.2`, a nested atomic `goalEntry` with `projectionRole: prerequisiteOnly`
  may declare `acquisitionPlacement: true`. This preserves its reviewed learning
  place as an internal presentation template. Only the backend's closed Personal
  Curriculum learning basis authorizes materialization, and an already visible
  target placement wins. Among multiple explicit templates, the goal's owning
  subject takes precedence over a foreign-subject fallback; wrapper depth
  across subjects is not curricular authority. Check the combined map for
  earlier demand and repair inappropriate authored timing. The flag creates
  no seeds, prerequisites or mastery.
  Unmarked support entries, global support declarations and broad support
  subtrees remain scope metadata; their location alone is never acquisition
  authority. Public views without Personal Curriculum metadata stay unchanged.
- Preserve effective atomic acyclicity and transitive minimality. When whole-unit
  sequencing is intended, additionally check the projected block graph at every
  relevant boundary: atomic acyclicity alone does not guarantee a unit order.
  Include inherited, indirect, cluster, hidden and cross-subject requirements.
- Optional workload claims need documented entry assumptions, supported effort
  estimates, reserve and overall/subject budgets. Count shared acquisition once;
  `weight` and raw goal counts are not time estimates. An assumed entry competency
  is not a learner mastery write.
- **Purpose (Product Owner, 11 November 2026).** A unit establishes solid,
  independent application of its promise before planned learning builds on it.
  Its assessments expose essential misconceptions; a gap leads to focused
  learning and a fresh equivalent assessment instead of a false completion. Unit
  boundaries follow meaningful conceptual transitions, not existing exams,
  target quotas, tidy headings or edge density; never add or remove edges to
  fit a cut. The ordinary grading contract applies: no hidden pass rule and no
  second stored outcome. This is a didactic requirement, not an access lock.
- **Unit validity rules (Product Owner, 11 November 2026).** Recognition (§4)
  is only sound if units hard-satisfy five rules, each with a fixed placement:
  - R1: every own ordinary content goal of a unit lies on a prerequisite path
    from at least one own assessment of that unit, directly or transitively; a
    path that leads only to a memory goal does not count. Validity condition of
    the unit definition, same class as acyclicity: hard from the first authored
    unit; a unit that violates it cannot be merged.
  - R2: for every own ordinary goal, at least one own assessment that requires
    it has reviewed evidence that its tasks and normal grading really test the
    goal. Milestone M5 (assessment approval and coverage metadata), with the
    current per-goal binding at M7; without it the curriculum does not reach
    the milestone.
  - R3: an assessment claims coverage only for goals it requires directly or
    transitively (coverage ⊆ Reach⁺ over the atomic prerequisite relation), so
    the assessment that actually tests a goal is among those the recognition
    rule waits for. Same placement as R1.
  - R4: every acquisition target has exactly one directly owning unit. Same
    placement as R1.
  - R5: a later re-cut of units or later edges never grant additional
    recognition to existing results; the contract is versioned. Runtime
    contract and precondition before implicit recognition is enabled in
    production.
  Implicit recognition exists only inside units whose contract satisfies R1–R4
  with reviewed coverage. Otherwise the unit's goals stay open, and the
  violation keeps the curriculum from reaching the milestone. A goal that no
  assessment covers therefore stays open *and* is a rule violation; it is never
  recognized for free ([Graph Definition §13.3](docs/concept/skill-graph/assessment-gated-completion.md#sec-why-graph-not-enough);
  `GD-U05`, `GD-U06`, `GD-U15`,
  `GD-A07`, `GD-A08`, `GD-A10`, `GD-A12`, `GD-A18`).
- **Resumption (Product Owner, 12 November 2026).** The learning-unit work is
  resumed; the working plan is
  `docs/dev/mathematics-physics-learning-units-plan.md` (Mathematics first, then
  Physics). The catalog of units is derived in-house, block by block, from the
  canonical graph and the design sheet; an external proposal is at most a
  recorded origin, never a contract. The whole-unit access question left open
  at that date was retired by B9 on 14 November 2026; only declared `after`
  orders whole-unit completion, without need or access locks.
- **Unit contract (Product Owner, 12 November 2026).** Learning units are part
  of the landscape and live in the existing placement layer; the Product Owner
  rejected a separate catalog file beside the landscape as a bolt-on and asked
  for a stable long-term contract, with a new schema version wherever one is
  needed. A unit is a `programUnits` entry of kind `learningUnit` (`id`,
  `label`, `labelEn`, `promise`, `promiseEn`, `revision`, optional
  `parentUnitId` naming the unit that requires it as a child). It owns atomic
  goals of the same landscape through `goalPlacements` of relation `own`,
  which carry exactly `goalId`, `unitId` and `relation` (no `context`). Reviewed coverage (R2) is the unit's
  `coverageReviews`, one entry per own assessment, hash-bound to that
  assessment's task, solution and rubric; goal objects carry nothing of the
  unit contract, so a review never changes a goal's bytes. The
  optional landscape field `learningUnitCoverage` is `partial` or `complete`.
  The declaration is closed: whole-unit prerequisites, access locks and waiver
  lists cannot be expressed. Curriculum-package contract 1.2
  (`contracts/curriculum-package/v1.2`) carries the units; builds under 1.0 or
  1.1 project them out completely, and published files of earlier versions
  stay unchanged. The completion profiles and scopes of contract 1.1 implement
  the superseded waiver semantics and remain in operation only until the
  runtime switches.
- **Unit validator (12 November 2026).** `npm --prefix app run
  validate:learning-units` enforces R1, R3 and R4 plus the declaration rules
  fail-closed for every learning unit in the canonical landscapes and runs in
  the required curriculum CI; only `learningUnitCoverage: "complete"` requires
  an owner for every atomic goal. Missing R2 evidence is reported, not
  rejected; it is not yet a milestone gate. R5 and implicit recognition are
  not implemented: a learning unit has no runtime effect, and every goal is
  still completed by its own explicit result. Never add an edge, weaken a goal
  description or cut a unit to turn the validator green; a finding is a
  subject-review task.
- **Bindings ignore the learning-unit layer (12 November 2026).** Evidence
  about goals, edges and assessments binds the landscape with its
  learning-unit layer projected out: without `programUnits` of kind
  `learningUnit`, without `goalPlacements` of relation `own` or towards a
  learning unit, and without `learningUnitCoverage`. It is the projection the
  package build applies for contracts 1.0 and 1.1; for a landscape without
  units it is the identity. Contracts V1 to V8 of the prerequisite-reduction
  proof and the input pin of the Mathematics duration layout fingerprint that
  projection; a registry record keeps its full digest. Since the freeze of
  12 November 2026 the active V9 registry binds single goals (§2.2), so
  neither a unit edit nor an unrelated goal change stales review reuse; the
  duration-layout pin still goes stale on any goal change in Mathematics.
  Carriers that predate learning units follow the same rule: a 1.0 or
  1.1 build and the legacy subject export (with its MEM/FWU roundtrip) ship
  the landscape without the layer; only contract 1.2 carries units. This was
  the recommendation presented to the Product Owner on 12 November 2026 after
  the first placement attempt broke the proof chain; the Product Owner asked
  to proceed. Never rebind a proof by hand and never touch a floor to place a
  unit. Record: `docs/dev/canonical-math-learning-unit-pilot-binding-review-2026-11-12.md`.

